        "orario": "20:12",
        "timestamp": 1777399953,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Insurgents reshaping Mali: Tuaregs seek autonomy, jihadists pursue caliphate across Sahel MALI Issued on: 28/04/2026 - 20:12 Annette Young is pleased to welcome Nina Wilen, Director of the Africa Programme at the Egmont Institute. According to Ms. Wilen, the insurgency in Mali reflects shifting power balances across the Sahel. What we are witnessing is not simply a surge in violence, but a reconfiguration of authority, where Tuareg rebels and jihadist groups, particularly the JNIM, have steadily expanded their operational capacity while state and international actors have lost ground. The recent coordinated attacks signal that they can strike at the heart of the state, yet they may not seek to govern it directly. Mali capital 'not under threat, but long term prospects do not look good', analyst says 28/04/2026 Africa Mali's tale of two insurgencies: 'Jihadists seek caliphate while Tuareg rebels pursue autonomy' 27/04/2026 Africa 'Unable to cope': Malian army, Wagner 'overstretched' by Tuareg separatists and jihadist militants 27/04/2026 Africa Russian mercenaries in Mali: Limited gains, reports of abuse 26/04/2026 Africa Malian Defence Minister Sadio Camara killed by rebels 26/04/2026 Africa Gunfire and blasts rock Mali: Army claims situation is under control 26/04/2026 Africa Armed groups claim attacks across Mali 26/04/2026 Africa Attacks on Bamako a 'dramatic setback' for Malian government 25/04/2026 Africa FLA claims attacks on Malian capital 25/04/2026 Africa"
